@startuml proto-sr04-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-sr04-timing.puml - (c) HC-SR04 timing, and exactly which numbers are
' measured and which are still open.
'
' From tb/tb_pe_soc_sr04.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real SRAM macro):
'
'   FAIL the firmware banked both measurements (dmem[10] = 01)
'   FAIL the model saw exactly 2 triggers (1)
'   FAIL the trigger is EXACTLY 600 clocks = 10.000 us (measured 601 = 10.017 us)
'   FAIL measurement 1: 1160 us is outside 1 % of the model's 5816 us
'   FAIL measurement 1: 1160 us is X mm, not 999 mm
'
'     measurement 0: echo 1160 us -> firmware 1160 us -> 199 mm  (expected 199)
'     measurement 1: echo 5816 us -> firmware 1160 us -> X mm    (expected 999)
' ===========================================================================

title HC-SR04 - timing, with the RED state stated rather than drawn over\n<font size=12>the 10 us trigger is 601 clocks where the act derives 600; the second echo is never measured</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "TRIG\npin 6" as TRIG
participant "the sensor\nmodel" as SENSE
participant "ECHO\npin 5, read back" as ECHO
participant "the 1 us counter\nport 4" as TICK

== measurement 0: this one works ==

CPU -> TRIG : TXPIN = SR_TRIG, PINOE = SR_TRIG
note over TRIG
  <b>4 * SR_TRIG + 4 = 4 * 149 + 4 = 600 clocks = 10.000 us</b>,
  which is the act's own derivation and the
  datasheet's 10 us minimum exactly.
end note
note over TRIG
  <b>MEASURED 601 CLOCKS = 10.0167 us.</b>

  One clock long. The device asks for a MINIMUM so
  this is harmless to a real sensor, and the check
  is an equality anyway because the act claims the
  COUNT rather than a datasheet floor.
end note
TRIG -> SENSE : the trigger
CPU -> TRIG : the counted delay, then release
SENSE -> ECHO : recovery, then 8 x 40 kHz, then ECHO HIGH
note over ECHO
  The firmware does not look at the 8 bursts: it
  cannot resynchronise to a burst it did not send.
  <b>THE ANSWER IS THE WIDTH OF THE PULSE, not a level,</b>
  which is what makes this act safe at 4 m - a width
  does not care when you looked at it.
end note
CPU -> ECHO : spin until the echo goes HIGH
note over ECHO
  <b>THE ECHO IS CAPTURED, NOT SAMPLED.</b> The counter
  is RESET at this edge and READ at the falling edge,
  so there is no 16-bit subtract - and this machine
  has no carry flag, so a subtract is a four-case
  analysis on bit 7 and a compare is the same
  analysis again. One reset removes both.
end note
CPU -> TICK : read the COUNTER, not the flag
note over TICK
  <b>THE 1 us COUNTER, read as a DIFFERENCE</b> - exactly as
  in freqmeter.pe, for the same reason: the clear-on-read
  flag on port 6 is ONE BIT, so a poll loop longer than a
  tick reports several ticks as one, and the loss is
  invisible in the result.

  Port 7 is the 4.33 us UART half-bit tick and is not a
  microsecond.
end note
TICK -> CPU : now
CPU -> CPU : t = the accumulated microseconds
note over CPU
  <b>MEASURED 1160 us</b> against the model's 1 160 us -
  <b>EXACT</b>, and the check is 1 % with a 2 us floor.
end note
CPU -> CPU : mm = us * 11/64, exactly
note over CPU
  us = 64q + r, so us*11/64 = 11q + floor(11r/64), exactly,
  because 11q is an integer. Four doublings and two adds
  for each multiply by eleven, no loop and no second
  temporary.
end note
note over CPU
  <b>1160 * 11/64 = 199.375 -> 199 mm. EXPECTED 199 mm.</b>
  ...
  <b>THE CONVERSION IS EXACT, AND IT IS THE ACT.</b> The
  testbench asserts it as an equality, not a window.
end note
CPU -> CPU : bank it, dmem[10] = 1

== measurement 1: this one does not ==

SENSE -> ECHO : the echo for the second target
note over SENSE
  <b>THE MODEL PREPARED 5 816 us -> expect 999 mm.</b>

  The large target is the one that makes the act worth
  running: 1 160 us fits in twelve bits and 5 816 us
  does not, so the pair exercises the ten-bit path AND
  the high byte. A single near target would prove the
  conversion works and say nothing about the case that
  could overflow - and an overflow in the high byte is
  invisible from the outside, because the answer would
  still be a plausible millimetre figure.
end note
note over SENSE
  <b>FAIL: THE MODEL SAW EXACTLY 1 TRIGGER, NOT 2.</b>

  The check carries its own explanation in the TB: "a
  trigger that is driven rather than pulsed shows up
  here" - which is the right way to write a failing
  check, because the reader learns the SHAPE of the
  defect from the message instead of going to hunt for
  it in the design.
end note
note over CPU
  <b>FAIL: dmem[10] = 01. THE FIRMWARE BANKED ONE
  MEASUREMENT.</b>
  ...
  and the second reading is still the FIRST one's:
    measurement 1: echo 5816 us -> firmware 1160 us
  ...
  <b>THE FAILURE IS UPSTREAM OF THE CONVERSION.</b> The
  arithmetic is exact on the measurement that ran, and
  every failing check is about the second measurement
  not happening rather than about the answer being
  wrong.
end note

== the open defect, and what is NOT claimed ==

note over CPU, ECHO
  <b>WHAT IS KNOWN:</b> the second measurement is lost, the bank holds one result,
  the model saw one trigger, and the trigger is one clock long.
  ...
  <b>WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED:</b> which instruction is wrong. The five failing checks
  say the second measurement does not happen; they do not say why, and a figure
  that drew a cause would be drawing a hypothesis presented as a finding.
  ...
  <b>WHERE TO LOOK FIRST:</b> the 601-vs-600 trigger, because it is a
  one-instruction error of exactly the kind this family has found six times, and
  because it sits in the path that issues the second trigger and re-arms for the
  second echo. Stated as a place to look, not as the fault.
  ...
  <b>WHAT IS NOT IN THE REGRESSION:</b> tb_pe_soc_sr04.v is tracked but NOT wired
  into regress/run_all.sh, so a green gate says nothing about this act in either
  direction. The act's own state has to be read from running it, and running it is
  how this page's numbers were obtained.
end note

note over CPU, ECHO
  <b>THE TIMING TABLE, marking what is measured.</b>
  ...
  | quantity | datasheet / derivation | measured | status |
  | trigger pulse | >= 10 us; the act derives 4*149+4 = 600 clocks = 10.000 us | <b>601 clocks = 10.0167 us</b> | FAIL, one clock long |
  | recovery | the sensor's own settling; SR_RECOV_LEN = 117, the stated 2 010 us | not separable in this run | open |
  | echo 1 | 1 160 us | <b>1 160 us</b> | PASS, exact |
  | echo 2 | 5 816 us | <b>1 160 us</b> | FAIL, the second measurement never ran |
  | mm from echo 1 | 1160 * 11/64 = 199.375 | <b>199 mm</b> | PASS, an equality |
  | mm from echo 2 | 5816 * 11/64 = 999.4 | <b>X</b> | FAIL, X is an unwritten byte |
  | measurements banked | 2 | <b>1</b> (dmem[10] = 01) | FAIL |
  ...
  <b>AND NOTE WHAT IS NOT IN THE TABLE.</b> No row says the conversion is wrong,
  because no conversion check failed. A red table that mixes a proven property with
  a broken one, and does not say which is which, is worse than a red table: the
  reader cannot tell whether to trust the arithmetic or distrust it.
end note

@enduml
